Explicit AVX2 Vector Operations in C++
When performance is paramount, reliance on compiler autovectorization is risky. Compilers frequently fail to vectorize loops due to pointer aliasing concerns or unknown loop boundary alignments. Engineers write architecture-specific intrinsic functions:
#include &lt;immintrin.h&gt;
#include &lt;iostream&gt;
#include &lt;vector&gt;

void vector_fma_avx2(const float* __restrict__ a,
                    const float* __restrict__ b,
                    const float* __restrict__ c,
                    float* __restrict__ out,
                    size_t n) {
    size_t i = 0;

    // Process 8 floats per iteration using 256-bit AVX2 registers
    for (; i + 8 &lt;= n; i += 8) {
        // Load vectors from memory
        __m256 va = _mm256_loadu_ps(&a[i]);
        __m256 vb = _mm256_loadu_ps(&b[i]);
        __m256 vc = _mm256_loadu_ps(&c[i]);

        // Fused Multiply-Add: out = (va * vb) + vc
        // Computes 8 FMAs (16 floating point operations) in 4-5 cycles latency
        __m256 vres = _mm256_fmadd_ps(va, vb, vc);

        // Store back to destination
        _mm256_storeu_ps(&out[i], vres);
    }

    // Scalar cleanup tail loop
    for (; i &lt; n; ++i) {
        out[i] = a[i] * b[i] + c[i];
    }
}


Alignment and Vector Loads

_mm256_loadu_ps: Unaligned load. Safe across any memory address, but incurs small penalty if crossing a 64-byte cache line boundary.
_mm256_load_ps: Aligned load. Requires the source address to be strictly 32-byte aligned (alignas(32) or posix_memalign). A non-aligned pointer will trigger a hardware general protection fault (SIGSEGV).

4. Cache Lines and False Sharing
CPU memory controllers do not fetch individual 4-byte floating-point numbers from system DRAM. All transfers between main memory and CPU caches occur in fixed chunks of 64 bytes, called cache lines.

The False Sharing Trap
When two distinct threads running on separate CPU cores write to independent variables that happen to sit within the same 64-byte cache line, the CPU hardware coherence engine (e.g., MESI protocol) must invalidate that entire cache line across both cores back and forth:
                  64-Byte Cache Line
+-----------------------------------+-----------------------------------+
| Thread 0 Counter: uint64_t (8 B)  | Thread 1 Counter: uint64_t (8 B)  |
+-----------------------------------+-----------------------------------+
                  ^                                   ^
                  |                                   |
          Core 0 writes                       Core 1 writes
          (Invalidates Core 1)                (Invalidates Core 0)
This phenomenon—false sharing—can slow down multi-threaded code by a factor of 50x to 100x, reducing parallel throughput below that of a single-threaded loop.


Remediation via Padding
// Bad: Threads write to adjacent elements in an array
struct BadThreadState {
    uint64_t counter; // 8 bytes -&gt; adjacent elements share a 64B line!
};

// Good: Align to 64 bytes (the hardware cache line size)
struct alignas(64) GoodThreadState {
    uint64_t counter;
    // 56 bytes of implicit padding inserted by the compiler
};

2. Multi-Dimensional Index Calculations
CUDA allows Grids and Blocks to be configured in 1D, 2D, or 3D using dim3. The hardware provides built-in read-only variables: - threadIdx.{x,y,z}: Thread coordinates within its block. - blockIdx.{x,y,z}: Block coordinates within the grid. - blockDim.{x,y,z}: Dimensions (thread count) of the block. - gridDim.{x,y,z}: Dimensions (block count) of the grid.

1D Global Index Calculation
For a 1D grid processing a flat vector of length \(N\):
\[\text{Global ID} = \text{blockIdx.x} \times \text{blockDim.x} + \text{threadIdx.x}\]
int idx = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;
if (idx &lt; n) {
    // Process element idx
}


2D Global Index Calculation (Matrix Coordinates)
For a 2D matrix with width \(W\) (columns) and height \(H\) (rows):
// Map block and thread coordinates to row and column
int col = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;
int row = blockIdx.y * blockDim.y + threadIdx.y;

// Linear memory index (Row-Major layout)
int linear_idx = row * width + col;

if (row &lt; height && col &lt; width) {
    matrix[linear_idx] = 42.0f;
}

4. Complete Worked Example: Vectorized FMA with Error Handling
A robust production CUDA implementation requires careful host allocation, device memory management, asynchronous stream synchronization, and error checking macros.
#include &lt;cuda_runtime.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;

// Macro for robust CUDA API error checking
#define CUDA_CHECK(call)                                                      \
    do {                                                                      \
        cudaError_t err = call;                                               \
        if (err != cudaSuccess) {                                             \
            fprintf(stderr, "CUDA error at %s:%d: %s\n", __FILE__, __LINE__,  \
                    cudaGetErrorString(err));                                 \
            exit(EXIT_FAILURE);                                               \
        }                                                                     \
    } while (0)

// Fused Multiply-Add Kernel: d[i] = a[i] * b[i] + c[i]
__global__ void fma_kernel(const float* __restrict__ a,
                          const float* __restrict__ b,
                          const float* __restrict__ c,
                          float* __restrict__ d,
                          int n) {
    int idx = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;
    if (idx &lt; n) {
        // Generates single hardware FFMA instruction
        d[idx] = a[idx] * b[idx] + c[idx];
    }
}

int main(void) {
    const int N = 1 &lt;&lt; 22; // ~4.19 million elements
    const size_t bytes = N * sizeof(float);

    // 1. Allocate Host Memory
    float *h_a = (float*)malloc(bytes);
    float *h_b = (float*)malloc(bytes);
    float *h_c = (float*)malloc(bytes);
    float *h_d = (float*)malloc(bytes);

    for (int i = 0; i &lt; N; ++i) {
        h_a[i] = 1.0f;
        h_b[i] = 2.0f;
        h_c[i] = 3.0f;
    }

    // 2. Allocate Device Memory
    float *d_a, *d_b, *d_c, *d_d;
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaMalloc(&d_a, bytes));
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaMalloc(&d_b, bytes));
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaMalloc(&d_c, bytes));
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaMalloc(&d_d, bytes));

    // 3. Copy inputs to Device
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaMemcpy(d_a, h_a, bytes, cudaMemcpyHostToDevice));
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaMemcpy(d_b, h_b, bytes, cudaMemcpyHostToDevice));
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaMemcpy(d_c, h_c, bytes, cudaMemcpyHostToDevice));

    // 4. Configure Grid & Launch Kernel
    const int threads_per_block = 256;
    const int blocks_per_grid = (N + threads_per_block - 1) / threads_per_block;

    fma_kernel&lt;&lt;&lt;blocks_per_grid, threads_per_block&gt;&gt;&gt;(d_a, d_b, d_c, d_d, N);

    // Check for launch errors and synchronize
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaGetLastError());
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaDeviceSynchronize());

    // 5. Copy results back
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaMemcpy(h_d, d_d, bytes, cudaMemcpyDeviceToHost));

    // Verify result: 1.0 * 2.0 + 3.0 = 5.0
    printf("Result verification: d[0] = %f, d[N-1] = %f\n", h_d[0], h_d[N-1]);

    // 6. Cleanup
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaFree(d_a));
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaFree(d_b));
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaFree(d_c));
    CUDA_CHECK(cudaFree(d_d));
    free(h_a); free(h_b); free(h_c); free(h_d);

    printf("Kernel execution completed successfully.\n");
    return 0;
}

5. Inspecting Intermediate PTX and Machine SASS
To understand what the GPU physically executes, compile to PTX (virtual assembly) and SASS (machine assembly):
# Generate PTX virtual assembly
nvcc -ptx -arch=sm_90 -O3 fma.cu -o fma.ptx

# Disassemble binary machine SASS
nvcc -arch=sm_90 -O3 fma.cu -o fma
cuobjdump --dump-sass fma &gt; fma.sass
Inside fma.sass, locate the inner loop body:
/*0030*/  LDG.E.SYS R2, [R4.64] ;      // Load 32-bit float from global memory
/*0040*/  LDG.E.SYS R3, [R6.64] ;      // Load 32-bit float from global memory
/*0050*/  FFMA R0, R2, R3, R0 ;        // Single-cycle Fused Multiply-Add instruction!
/*0060*/  STG.E.SYS [R8.64], R0 ;      // Store result back to global memory
Notice FFMA: The hardware fused multiply and addition into a single instruction without an intermediate rounding step, preserving numeric precision while doubling arithmetic throughput.

4. Case Study: Step-by-Step Optimization of Matrix Transpose
Matrix transpose (\(B_{j,i} = A_{i,j}\)) is the ultimate memory benchmark: if you read \(A\) row-wise (coalesced), you must write \(B\) column-wise (uncoalesced), or vice-versa.
Achieving near-peak memory bandwidth requires staging tiles through shared memory.

Step 1: Naive Transpose (Uncoalesced Writes)
__global__ void transpose_naive(const float* __restrict__ in,
                                float* __restrict__ out,
                                int width, int height) {
    int x = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x; // column
    int y = blockIdx.y * blockDim.y + threadIdx.y; // row

    if (x &lt; width && y &lt; height) {
        // Coalesced Read: Adjacent threads have adjacent x (consecutive in memory)
        float val = in[y * width + x];

        // UNCOALESCED Write: Adjacent threads have adjacent y, strided by height!
        out[x * height + y] = val;
    }
}
Benchmark Result on A100: Reaches only ~18% of peak global memory bandwidth due to serialized uncoalesced writes.



Step 2: Coalesced Transpose via Shared Memory (With Bank Conflicts)
We load a 2D tile from global memory into shared memory coalesced, synchronize threads, and then write the transposed tile out to global memory coalesced:
#define TILE_DIM 32
#define BLOCK_ROWS 8

__global__ void transpose_coalesced_conflicted(const float* __restrict__ in,
                                               float* __restrict__ out,
                                               int width, int height) {
    __shared__ float tile[TILE_DIM][TILE_DIM];

    int x = blockIdx.x * TILE_DIM + threadIdx.x;
    int y = blockIdx.y * TILE_DIM + threadIdx.y;

    // 1. Read input tile coalesced into shared memory
    for (int j = 0; j &lt; TILE_DIM; j += BLOCK_ROWS) {
        if (x &lt; width && (y + j) &lt; height) {
            tile[threadIdx.y + j][threadIdx.x] = in[(y + j) * width + x];
        }
    }

    __syncthreads();

    // Transpose block coordinates for writing
    x = blockIdx.y * TILE_DIM + threadIdx.x;
    y = blockIdx.x * TILE_DIM + threadIdx.y;

    // 2. Write transposed tile coalesced to global memory
    for (int j = 0; j &lt; TILE_DIM; j += BLOCK_ROWS) {
        if (x &lt; height && (y + j) &lt; width) {
            // Reading tile[threadIdx.x][threadIdx.y + j] triggers a 32-WAY BANK CONFLICT!
            out[(y + j) * height + x] = tile[threadIdx.x][threadIdx.y + j];
        }
    }
}
The Problem: While global memory reads and writes are now 100% coalesced, reading tile[threadIdx.x][...] causes all 32 threads in a warp to access the same bank simultaneously (bank index = (threadIdx.x * 32 + ...) % 32 = 0), creating a catastrophic 32-way shared memory bank conflict!



Step 3: Conflict-Free Transpose with Shared Memory Padding
We eliminate bank conflicts with a single architectural stroke: add one element of padding to each row of the shared memory tile:
// Add +1 column padding: shifts each row by 1 bank!
__shared__ float tile[TILE_DIM][TILE_DIM + 1];
With [TILE_DIM][TILE_DIM + 1]: - Row 0 starts at Bank 0 (0 % 32). - Row 1 starts at Bank 1 (33 % 32 = 1). - Row 2 starts at Bank 2 (66 % 32 = 2). - Reading column threadIdx.x now distributes the 32 threads across 32 unique banks. Bank conflicts drop to zero!


Full Production Code
#include &lt;cuda_runtime.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

#define TILE_DIM 32
#define BLOCK_ROWS 8

__global__ void transpose_optimal(const float* __restrict__ in,
                                  float* __restrict__ out,
                                  int width, int height) {
    // 32 x 33 shared memory array: +1 padding eliminates all bank conflicts
    __shared__ float tile[TILE_DIM][TILE_DIM + 1];

    int x = blockIdx.x * TILE_DIM + threadIdx.x;
    int y = blockIdx.y * TILE_DIM + threadIdx.y;

    // 1. Coalesced Global Read -&gt; Conflict-Free Shared Write
    #pragma unroll
    for (int j = 0; j &lt; TILE_DIM; j += BLOCK_ROWS) {
        if (x &lt; width && (y + j) &lt; height) {
            tile[threadIdx.y + j][threadIdx.x] = in[(y + j) * width + x];
        }
    }

    __syncthreads();

    // Transpose tile destination mapping
    x = blockIdx.y * TILE_DIM + threadIdx.x;
    y = blockIdx.x * TILE_DIM + threadIdx.y;

    // 2. Conflict-Free Shared Read -&gt; Coalesced Global Write
    #pragma unroll
    for (int j = 0; j &lt; TILE_DIM; j += BLOCK_ROWS) {
        if (x &lt; height && (y + j) &lt; width) {
            out[(y + j) * height + x] = tile[threadIdx.x][threadIdx.y + j];
        }
    }
}

1. Dissecting the Naive GEMM: Why is it Slow?
In a naive GEMM implementation, each thread computes a single output element \(C_{i,j}\) by performing a dot product between row \(i\) of matrix \(A\) and column \(j\) of matrix \(B\).
Matrix A (M x K)              Matrix B (K x N)              Matrix C (M x N)
+-------------------+         +-----+---+-----+             +-----+---+-----+
|                   |         |     | | |     |             |     | | |     |
|-------------------| (Row i) |     | | |     |             |     | | |     |
|                   |    x    |     | | |     |      =      +-----+-v-+-----+
|                   |         |     | | |     |             |     |C_ij|    |
+-------------------+         +-----+-v-+-----+             +-----+---+-----+
                                    (Col j)

The Naive CUDA Kernel
__global__ void gemm_naive(const float* __restrict__ A,
                           const float* __restrict__ B,
                           float* __restrict__ C,
                           int M, int N, int K) {
    // Determine row and column for this thread
    int row = blockIdx.y * blockDim.y + threadIdx.y;
    int col = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;

    if (row &lt; M && col &lt; N) {
        float sum = 0.0f;
        // Compute dot product along dimension K
        for (int k = 0; k &lt; K; ++k) {
            sum += A[row * K + k] * B[k * N + col];
        }
        C[row * N + col] = sum;
    }
}


Performance Autopsy
Consider running this kernel on an NVIDIA A100 (\(M = N = K = 4096\)): 1. Flop Count: \(2 \times M \times N \times K = 2 \times 4096^3 \approx 137.4 \times 10^9 \text{ FLOPs}\). 2. Global Memory Loads: - For every step of loop \(k\), thread \((i, j)\) fetches \(A_{i,k}\) (4 bytes) and \(B_{k,j}\) (4 bytes) from off-chip DRAM. - Total bytes loaded from DRAM across all threads = \(M \times N \times K \times 2 \times 4 \text{ B} \approx 549.7 \times 10^9 \text{ Bytes}\) (~550 GB). 3. Operational Intensity: \[I_{\text{naive}} = \frac{2 \text{ FLOPs}}{8 \text{ Bytes}} = 0.25 \text{ FLOPs/Byte}\] 4. The Bottleneck: - On the A100, the ridge point is \(I^* = 9.6 \text{ FLOPs/Byte}\). - At \(I = 0.25\), the kernel is trapped deep within the Memory-Bound regime. - Maximum attainable performance: \[P_{\text{naive}} = 2,039 \text{ GB/s} \times 0.25 \text{ FLOPs/Byte} \approx 509.7 \text{ GFLOP/s}\] - Result: \(509.7 \text{ GFLOP/s} / 19,500 \text{ GFLOP/s} = \mathbf{2.6\%}\) of theoretical peak. In reality, uncoalesced memory traffic on matrix \(B\) drags actual performance down to **1.3%** (260 GFLOP/s).

3. The 2D Tiled CUDA Kernel
#include &lt;cuda_runtime.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

#define TILE_WIDTH 32

__global__ void gemm_tiled_shared_mem(const float* __restrict__ A,
                                      const float* __restrict__ B,
                                      float* __restrict__ C,
                                      int M, int N, int K) {
    // 2D shared memory cache tiles
    __shared__ float s_A[TILE_WIDTH][TILE_WIDTH];
    __shared__ float s_B[TILE_WIDTH][TILE_WIDTH];

    int tx = threadIdx.x;
    int ty = threadIdx.y;

    // Output coordinate in matrix C
    int row = blockIdx.y * TILE_WIDTH + ty;
    int col = blockIdx.x * TILE_WIDTH + tx;

    float acc = 0.0f;

    // Number of phase tiles needed along dimension K
    int num_tiles = (K + TILE_WIDTH - 1) / TILE_WIDTH;

    for (int t = 0; t &lt; num_tiles; ++t) {
        // 1. Collaborative loading of Tile A into shared memory
        int a_col = t * TILE_WIDTH + tx;
        if (row &lt; M && a_col &lt; K) {
            s_A[ty][tx] = A[row * K + a_col];
        } else {
            s_A[ty][tx] = 0.0f;
        }

        // 2. Collaborative loading of Tile B into shared memory
        int b_row = t * TILE_WIDTH + ty;
        if (b_row &lt; K && col &lt; N) {
            s_B[ty][tx] = B[b_row * N + col];
        } else {
            s_B[ty][tx] = 0.0f;
        }

        // 3. Barrier synchronization: ensure entire tile is present in SRAM
        __syncthreads();

        // 4. Accumulate partial dot product from shared memory
        #pragma unroll
        for (int k = 0; k &lt; TILE_WIDTH; ++k) {
            acc += s_A[ty][k] * s_B[k][tx];
        }

        // 5. Barrier synchronization: ensure computation finishes before next tile load
        __syncthreads();
    }

    // Write final accumulated result to global memory
    if (row &lt; M && col &lt; N) {
        C[row * N + col] = acc;
    }
}

Tensor Cores & WMMA: The Second Worklog
Starting with the Volta architecture, NVIDIA GPUs include specialized mixed-precision execution hardware: Tensor Cores.

The Tensor Core Advantage

Conventional CUDA cores execute 1 FMA (Fused Multiply-Add = 2 FLOPs) per cycle.
Tensor Cores compute full matrix multiply-accumulate operations (\(D = A imes B + C\)) on small matrix fragments (e.g. \(16 imes 16 imes 16\)) in hardware every cycle.
Speedup: ~5-10x higher theoretical compute density compared to FP32 CUDA cores.



Direct Programming via WMMA (Warp Matrix Multiply Accumulate)
CUDA provides the nvcuda::wmma C++ API to program Tensor Cores directly from device code:
#include &lt;mma.h&gt;
using namespace nvcuda;

// Cooperative 16x16x16 matrix multiply across 32 threads in a warp
__global__ void wmma_gemm_kernel(const half* A, const half* B, float* C, int M, int N, int K) {
    // Declare WMMA fragments
    wmma::fragment&lt;wmma::matrix_a, 16, 16, 16, half, wmma::row_major&gt; a_frag;
    wmma::fragment&lt;wmma::matrix_b, 16, 16, 16, half, wmma::col_major&gt; b_frag;
    wmma::fragment&lt;wmma::accumulator, 16, 16, 16, float&gt; c_frag;

    wmma::fill_fragment(c_frag, 0.0f);

    for (int k = 0; k &lt; K; k += 16) {
        // Load sub-matrices cooperatively across the warp
        wmma::load_matrix_sync(a_frag, A + (blockIdx.y * 16 * K) + k, K);
        wmma::load_matrix_sync(b_frag, B + (blockIdx.x * 16 * K) + k, K);

        // Hardware Tensor Core Matrix Multiply
        wmma::mma_sync(c_frag, a_frag, b_frag, c_frag);
    }

    // Store accumulator fragment back to global memory
    wmma::store_matrix_sync(C + (blockIdx.y * 16 * N) + (blockIdx.x * 16), c_frag, N, wmma::mem_row_major);
}

2. The kernel
acc[N] with full unrolling keeps N partial sums live at the same time, which is a controllable register-pressure knob. BOUNDS is injected by the build script.
// kernel.cu — one register-hungry kernel, compiled with and without __launch_bounds__
#ifndef N
#define N 128
#endif
#ifndef BOUNDS
#define BOUNDS
#endif
extern "C" __global__ void BOUNDS
poly_eval(const float* __restrict__ x, float* __restrict__ y, int n) {
    int i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;
    if (i &gt;= n) return;
    float v = x[i];
    float acc[N];                       // N live partial sums -&gt; wants many registers
#pragma unroll
    for (int k = 0; k &lt; N; ++k) acc[k] = v * (k + 1);
#pragma unroll
    for (int it = 0; it &lt; 8; ++it)
#pragma unroll
        for (int k = 0; k &lt; N; ++k) acc[k] = fmaf(acc[k], v, acc[(k + 7) & (N - 1)]);
    float s = 0.f;
#pragma unroll
    for (int k = 0; k &lt; N; ++k) s += acc[k];
    y[i] = s;
}

Verification, Profiling, and Benchmarking Harness
Every kernel submission must pass a three-tiered validation suite:

1. Correctness Verification
Compare kernel output tensors against double-precision PyTorch / FP64 reference outputs across edge-case dimensions, non-multiple-of-32 matrix sizes, and extreme numerical scales (\(10^{-5}\) to \(10^5\)). Maximum allowable relative error:
\[\frac{\|Y_{\text{kernel}} - Y_{\text{ref}}\|_2}{\|Y_{\text{ref}}\|_2} &lt; 10^{-3}\]


2. CUDA Event Benchmarking
Measure kernel execution time using cudaEventRecord over 1,000 warm iterations, discarding outliers:
cudaEvent_t start, stop;
cudaEventCreate(&start);
cudaEventCreate(&stop);

cudaEventRecord(start);
for (int i = 0; i &lt; 1000; ++i) {
    launch_kernel&lt;&lt;&lt;grid, block&gt;&gt;&gt;(...);
}
cudaEventRecord(stop);
cudaEventSynchronize(stop);

float ms = 0;
cudaEventElapsedTime(&ms, start, stop);
printf("Average execution time: %f ms\n", ms / 1000.0f);
